    "result": "I implemented `parse_line` in `solution.py` following `spec.md`, and wrote 60 pytest tests in `tests/test_solution.py`; `python -m pytest -q tests` passes all 60. The implementation splits the work into three small helpers: `_check_sep` validates the separator, `_read_quoted` decodes a quoted field (handling doubled quotes and the closing-quote check), and `_read_unquoted` reads a plain field and rejects `\"`, `\\r` and `\\n`. `parse_line` checks types first with `TypeError`, then loops over fields, skipping one separator per step, so `\"\"`, `\"a,\"` and `\",\"` yield the expected field counts. The tests are parametrized over the spec's examples and the error cases: wrong types, bad separators, unclosed quotes, text after a closing quote, stray quotes and line breaks outside quotes. I did not measure coverage, CRAP scores, mutation score or bandit results, since those gates are run separately and I only had the one allowed test command. The helper split should keep each function's complexity low, and I expect the tests to cover most branches, but I have not verified those gates.",
